On Tuesday, July 24, 2012 11:45:39 AM UTC+4, aionys wrote:
>
> On Tue, Jul 24, 2012 at 1:09 AM, Gleki Arxokuna <
> gleki.is.my.name@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>> Well, it's like BAI and FA.
>> You can use free order of arguments like in FA but there is no need to 
>> memorise the order of sumti.
>> So actually it's like using BAI but you can't use such case tag if it's 
>> not in gismu definition.
>>
>> So imagine that
>> dunda = x1 gives x2 to x3
>> but instead of ko'a dunda ko'e ko'i you don't memorise the order
>> but say something like
>>
>> {to'o ko'a dunda ri'i ko'e fa'a ko'i}
>> and there is no need in memorisng the order of arguments.
>> Your intuition tells you that ko'e (x2 of dunda) is like direct object in 
>> European languages therefore you use {ri'i}, you give the gift TOWARDS 
>> someone so it's {fa'a}.
>>
>> BUT IN N-PARADIGM YOU CAN'T USE ANY OTHER *BAI* except those three.
>>
>> Therefore they are worse than BAI (that can be used with any gismu) but 
>> better than FA (where you must remember the order).
>>
>> So it's an attempt to introduce cases present in many world languages 
>> like German or even English (consider "they->"them" resulting in accusative 
>> case). This might make language more naturalistic but I wonder whether 
>> those cases are universal and intuitive (whether they can eliminate the 
>> need in memorising the order of places)..
>>
>
>  

> I don't see how it's of much benefit. While it arguably removes the need 
> to remember the order of places, with such a restriction, it still requires 
> knowing the places, and additionally, it requires knowing the /words/ for 
> the places, which would be different for every brivla.
>
Anyway it removes one stumbling block. You can write "x1 dunda x2 towards 
x3" or "towards x3 x1 (subject) gives x2 (gift)".
Yes, it doesn't remove other drawbacks (which are actually present in all 
European languages so they are not drawbacks, they are "normal").
 

> The list of words on the linked page is also far from exhaustive and each 
> word is itself extremely general. These considerations say to me that our 
> BAI are much more extensive in their utility and scope, for what I believe 
> to be obvious reasons. In short, I would that our solution, with FA and 
> BAI, is a better one than the N-paradigm, but that is of course merely my 
> opinion.
>
> As to whether it's is "universal" or "intuitive", I have no idea and will 
> leave that to the linguists in our group to ponder, but I'm leaning rather 
> heavily to "no" on both counts.
>
N-paradigm is kinda half-way to destructing sumti places at all. If 
loglanists made all gismu having one sumti place only and expressed other 
places using either BAI or separate gismu that would make sense (if they 
were able to retain the integrity of the language). And that would be 
extremely interesting (for examination) but I guess it can be done within 
Lojban as I'm currently unaware of BAI in Loglan. And after all Loglan is 
rather dead and there is nobody to ask about it.

But let's wait for others to say a word about this paradigm.

 
>
